// An arithmetic policy on an array's ELEMENT type removes the whole-array
// store row. The same policy on the ARRAY does not. Reduced from
// `samples/cli/collections/matrix_multiply`.
//
//   [i32; 3]               <- [1, 2, 3]   checks
//   [u8; 3]                <- [1, 2, 3]   checks
//   [i32; 3] in Wrapping   <- [1, 2, 3]   checks    <- policy on the ARRAY
//   [i32 in Wrapping; 3]   <- [1, 2, 3]   OMITTED   <- policy on the ELEMENT
//   [i32 in Saturating; 3] <- [1, 2, 3]   OMITTED
//
// Nesting is NOT what carries it, which is the reading the sample's shape
// invites: `matrix_multiply` stores `[[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]]` into a
// `[[i32 in Wrapping; 3]; 3]`, but a FLAT `[i32 in Wrapping; 3]` omits just the
// same, and a nested `[[i32; 3]; 3]` takes the identical nested literal. Only
// the element policy moves the outcome.
//
// Storing ONE element (`self.grid[0][0] = 1`) checks. Storing one ROW
// (`self.grid[0] = [1, 2, 3]`) omits at a neighbouring phase,
// `structural field store: scalar field type: indexed element destination`,
// so the gap is whole-composite stores rather than element stores.
//
// The phase names a scalar field, and the field is an array. The trace mark is
// set on entry to the structural field store and the route then leaves through
// a later `?` without re-marking, so the label reports where it ENTERED, not
// what declined -- read it as "the structural field store declined", nothing
// finer.
//
// SAME FAMILY as `tests/omega/pending/structs/policy_field_record_local_read`:
// there, an arithmetic policy on a record's FIELD removes the field-read row
// for a local of that record; here, a policy on an array's ELEMENT removes the
// whole-array store row. In both, the policy on the COMPOSITE is fine and the
// policy on the MEMBER is not. Worth one Terminal answer rather than two.
use omega_language_std::console;
use omega::language::core::binding;

data Main {
    console: Binding<Console>;
    row: [i32 in Wrapping; 3];
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) reaches Console {
    self.row = [1, 2, 3];
    self.console.exit_process(0);
}
